'That comment is the whole bug, and I have to be careful not to write it up as stupidity, because it is the reasoning of somebody who tested it on hardware that forgave them. The claim is that ready is what the consumer waits on, so the item cannot be read too early. But ready is not published atomically at all. It is a plain bool in the same cache line as the item, and the only atomic operations in the sequence are relaxed — so the guarantee being leaned on is borrowed from a hardware promise that exists on x86 and not on arm64. The 4 percent benchmark that justified the choice was run on the build farm, and the comment says the verification was TSan on that same farm, which means the fix was tested on the one architecture where the bug cannot appear. Now the failure rate has to fit the mechanism, and this is where it stops being plausible and starts being likely. A missing release does not corrupt anything most of the time, because the payload write usually lands long before another core can poll the line. The bad case needs the store buffer to still hold the slot write at the moment a reader on the second core polls it, which is most likely when that write is expensive: a cold line, a page not yet resident, or a producer that just missed on the item itself. On a busy us-east host under memory pressure that window opens a little more often, and a little more often, times a week, is an incident. So the cadence, the architecture, the absence of a crash and the fact that a restart clears it all point the same way, which is the most a diagnosis without a reproducer can honestly ask for.',
        text: 'Checking whether anything upstream already orders the handoff. If it does, I am about to recommend a barrier that buys nothing.',
